  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows dry weather across \neast central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local  \nobservations indicate clear skies over the region. Analysis charts\nshow the cold front that moved through yesterday and last night \nto the southeast of east central Florida. Building high pressure \n(~1022mb) is situated over the Deep South. Temperatures have \ncooled behind the cold front and are currently in the low to mid \n80s with dew points in the low 60s to low 70s. Winds have been \nsomewhat breezy at times and are currently from the north at \n6-12mph with gusts to 15-22mph. \n\nDry weather is expected to persist for the remainder of today and \ntonight as high pressure builds over the Deep South. Expect low \ntemperatures in the low 60s to low 70s tonight (warmest along the \ncoast) with clear skies. \n\nTuesday-Wednesday... The dry weather will continue on Tuesday \nbefore moisture begins to increase over east central Florida as \nnortherly flow veers onshore. A longwave ridge is expected to \nbuild over the eastern CONUS through the forecast period. At the \nsurface, high pressure (~1020mb) is expected to build over the \nsoutheastern US through midweek. Guidance shows PWAT values \nincreasing to 1.0- 1.3\" by Wednesday. Isolated onshore moving \nshowers are forecast on Wednesday (mainly to the east and south of\nthe Orlando metro. The potential exists for isolated gusty \nshowers with moderate rainfall. Seasonable afternoon highs in the \nmid to upper 80s with seasonable to above normal (~1F-3F+ degrees)\nlow temperatures. \n\nThursday-Sunday... Deterministic models have come into slightly \nbetter agreement on the mid to long term forecast, however, there \nare still deviations in the different LREF-clusters' potential \nmodel solutions Friday and into the weekend. A longwave ridge is \nforecast to build over the southeastern US on Thursday with the \napex of the ridge forecast to reach Canada. At the surface, the \nhigh is forecast to shift east over the southeastern US and the \nsouthwest Atlantic Friday into Saturday. There still remains \nuncertainty in the strength of the mid/upper level trough/low that\nis forecast to deepen over the eastern CONUS Friday into Saturday\nwith the LREF-clusters showing clusters that have the trough \ndeepen all the way to the Deep south and the other clusters \ndeepening the trough to the Tennessee Valley, as well as the \nCarolinas. At the surface, a cold front is forecast to extend from\nlow pressure over the northeastern US and move east-southeast \nacross east central Florida Friday into Saturday. Rain chances \nincrease most days through the forecast period with isolated to \nnumerous showers (mainly south of Cape Canaveral on Thursday) and \nisolated to widely scattered lighting storms forecast each day. \nWinds are forecast to become breezy to windy from the northeast \nSaturday and Sunday. The probability of wind gusts to 35mph or \ngreater is at 30-70%+ on Saturday (highest probabilities north of \nthe Treasure Coast) and 40-80%+ on Sunday. Seasonable to above \nnormal temperatures on Thursday and Friday are forecast to cool \ninto the weekend behind the cold front.",
